#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176965 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial\n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large!\n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8$. If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 8x^2 + 24x + 16 \\\\[5pt] &= 8(x^2 + 3x + 2) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2)\n$$\n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient\n\n$$\n(1+2=3)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the linear term as\n\n$$\n3x=x+2x\n$$\n\nand factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3x + 2 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + x + 2x + 2 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + x) + (2x + 2) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 1) + 2(x + 1) &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 2)(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x^{2} + 24x + 16 &=  \\\\\n8(x^{2} + 3x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= 8(x + 2)(x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7336a5d6-0b38-5ba4-b278-aaac6a402ece" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1722 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
